  "findings": [
    "Legacy P1A FCR materially undercounted valid first actions because its default oracle preferred read even for command-native tasks and conflated runtime tool failure with argument non-dispatchability.",
    "The corrected six-task P1A corpus has 36/36 FCR-v2 and therefore has no wrong-tool discrimination headroom; it cannot justify semantic tool hiding or domain projection.",
    "13 of 19 P1A rejected calls are macOS cat -A portability failures. The dominant measured friction is command construction portability, not tool discovery or the largest tool-schema rows.",
    "B/D use slightly more median uncached input tokens than A but show fewer rejects, repair sequences and serial barriers at equal correctness/FCR-v2. Whole-run execution efficiency therefore must not be reduced to schema bytes or tool count.",
    "Current edit_file compact schema already exposes the snapshot/version precondition that caused historical edit failures, so archived edit failures must not be assumed to reproduce on current main."
  ],
  "next_experiment": {
    "name": "eev2.1-execute-command-portability-and-wrong-tool-holdout",
    "keep_all_healthy_tools_visible": true,
    "candidate_change": "Add only a short load-bearing execute_command command-field contract stating that commands execute on the actual host shell/toolset, must not assume GNU-only flags, and should prefer portable forms or inspect host/tool facts when portability matters.",
